---
/**
 * Button — the only button.
 *
 * WHY THIS EXISTS. `background: var(--c-teal)` was hand-typed in NINE separate
 * files: Footer, Nav, Base, Compare, Glossary and Sector layouts, and three
 * index pages. Nine independent definitions of one control is exactly the
 * duplication this rebuild exists to remove, and it is how a design system dies
 * quietly: not by anyone deciding to diverge, but by nobody having a component
 * to reach for.
 *
 * PARITY WITH FIGMA. The variant axes here are the same axes as the `Button`
 * component set in the Figma library (wJ9DK6ByFUN6rWe0CpCVPU): Style x Size.
 * They are the same control described twice, so they must not drift. If a
 * variant is added in one place it is added in both.
 *
 * ACCESSIBILITY. `size="sm"` resolves to --btn-h-sm, which is 44px. That is the
 * WCAG 2.2 target-size floor, not a style preference, so it is the smallest a
 * control may ever be. Do not add a smaller size without reading 2.5.8 first.
 *
 * Renders <a> when `href` is given and <button> otherwise, because a control
 * that navigates is a link and a control that acts is a button. Getting this
 * wrong breaks keyboard behaviour, middle-click, and the screen-reader
 * announcement, none of which CSS can put back.
 */
interface Props {
  href?: string;
  variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary';
  size?: 'md' | 'sm';
  /**
   * `reset` is here because the PPN 002 calculator has a real reset control and
   * a component that cannot express it forces the call site to hand-roll a
   * button, which is exactly how `.calc__reset` came to exist.
   */
  type?: 'button' | 'submit' | 'reset';
  /**
   * Forwarded because the contact and partner forms hold their submit button by
   * id from a script, to disable it against a double submit. Without this the
   * two forms could not be converted, and both kept a second button recipe
   * alive for the sake of one attribute.
   */
  id?: string;
  /** Accessible name when the visible label is not sufficient on its own. */
  label?: string;
  /**
   * target/rel are forwarded because five sign-in links could NOT be converted
   * without them: they carry target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer", and a
   * component that silently dropped those would open in the same tab and lose
   * the noopener protection. A component that cannot express what its call
   * sites need is not a component, it is a narrower thing pretending.
   *
   * rel defaults to noopener noreferrer whenever target is _blank, so the
   * protection cannot be forgotten at a call site.
   */
  target?: string;
  rel?: string;
  /**
   * The magnetic lean, restored 2026-08-03. Defaults to ON for the primary
   * variant and OFF for the secondary, so no call site has to remember it.
   *
   * WHY IT IS AUTOMATIC RATHER THAN AN ATTRIBUTE. The legacy site opted in with
   * a hand-written `data-magnetic`, and the result was 3 on /contact, 2 on the
   * homepage, 1 on /pricing and 1 on /about — which reads as a considered
   * distribution and is really just wherever somebody remembered. The primary
   * button IS the emphasised control; that is what the variant means. Tying the
   * effect to the variant makes the distribution a property of the design
   * system instead of a property of who edited which page last.
   *
   * The escape hatch exists for the case the default cannot see: a primary
   * button inside something that already moves, where two motions on one
   * element compete. Pass `magnetic={false}` and say why at the call site.
   */
  magnetic?: boolean;
  class?: string;
}

const {
  href,
  variant = 'primary',
  size = 'md',
  type = 'button',
  label,
  target,
  rel,
  id,
  magnetic,
  class: className = '',
} = Astro.props;

const resolvedRel = rel ?? (target === '_blank' ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined);
const Tag = href ? 'a' : 'button';
const classes = ['btn', `btn--${variant}`, `btn--${size}`, className].filter(Boolean).join(' ');
/* `??` and not `||`: `magnetic={false}` has to survive, and `||` would discard
   it as falsy and silently re-enable the effect the call site just turned off. */
const isMagnetic = magnetic ?? variant === 'primary';
---

<Tag
  class={classes}
  id={id}
  href={href}
  type={href ? undefined : type}
  aria-label={label}
  target={target}
  rel={resolvedRel}
  data-magnetic={isMagnetic ? '' : undefined}
>
  <slot />
</Tag>

<style>
  /* Unlayered on purpose: Astro's scoped component styles rank above every
     cascade layer, so a component beats base and layout without anyone
     reaching for !important. That is the whole point of the layer order
     declared in tokens.css. */
  .btn {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    padding-inline: 1.5rem;
    /* ── A FLOOR ON WIDTH, AND WHY THE LAST INVESTIGATION MISSED THIS ────────
     *
     * The owner reported unequal buttons twice. The note below answered the
     * first report by measuring HEIGHT, found every .btn to be 54px with
     * identical padding, type and border, and concluded the difference was
     * optical. The heights were right and the conclusion was wrong, because the
     * difference is WIDTH.
     *
     * Measured in the hero: "Request access" 152px, "Learn more" 127px. A 25px
     * mismatch on the pair a reader looks at first. Every button hugged its own
     * label, so a shorter word made a smaller control, and a pair of controls
     * offering equal choices looked like a big one and a small one.
     *
     * A FLOOR RATHER THAN A FIXED WIDTH. Buttons still grow for long labels —
     * "Try the PPN 002 calculator" must not be truncated or wrapped to match
     * something shorter. What the floor removes is the small-looking control:
     * every short label now lands on the same width, which is the case the eye
     * actually reads as an error.
     *
     * 9.5rem is the measured width of the primary in the hero, so the pair the
     * owner named resolves to 152px and 152px rather than to a new third size.
     *
     * The lesson is worth more than the fix: "are they the same size" was
     * answered by measuring one dimension. Measure the one that differs. */
    min-width: var(--btn-min-w, 9.5rem);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--btn-fs);
    font-weight: var(--btn-fw);
    line-height: 1;
    text-decoration: none;
    white-space: nowrap;
    cursor: pointer;
    border: 1px solid transparent;
    /* ── THE LAYER THE SWEEP SITS ON, DECLARED RATHER THAN INHERITED ─────────
     *
     * Both of these used to be on `.btn--primary` alone, because the sweep was
     * on `.btn--primary` alone. The sweep is on every button now (see THE SWEEP
     * below), so the context it needs belongs to every button.
     *
     * `position: relative` is the containing block the `::after` is absolutely
     * positioned against, and it was NEVER STATED on this control. It worked
     * anyway, by accident: `translate` below is `0px 0px` rather than `none`,
     * and a non-none `translate` establishes a containing block. So removing the
     * magnet would have silently detached the sweep from the button and stretched
     * it across the nearest positioned ancestor. `.fc .btn--primary` in
     * styles/effects.css states it too, for one button on one section, which is
     * how the defect stayed hidden.
     *
     * `isolation: isolate` is the stacking context the `z-index: -1` on that
     * layer resolves against. TWO pseudo-elements sit on this control at
     * negative z-index — the sweep, and the CTA pulse in styles/effects.css —
     * and both depend on painting ABOVE the button's own fill and BELOW its
     * label. Without an explicit context they resolve against whatever ancestor
     * happens to establish one, which on a section with an ambient field means
     * behind the section. */
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    /* ── THE TWO SCALES, NAMED SO THE PREFERENCE HAS ONE PLACE TO REACH ──────
     *
     * Every movement this control makes is composed from four values: these two
     * and --lift-btn / --lift-card from tokens.css. The reduced-motion block at
     * the foot of this file rebinds all four to their identity values, which is
     * what lets one rule honour the preference for every state instead of a
     * media query per state. See that block for why the override could not be a
     * list of selectors and could not be a layer.
     *
     * They are LITERALS rather than tokens because they are this control's own
     * geometry, not a site-wide scale: nothing else on the site scales on hover,
     * so a token would be a promise of sharing that is false on the day it is
     * written. 1.05 is live's measured hover; 0.98 is the platform press value
     * styles/motion.css restored from sovereign-primitives.css:44-46. */
    --btn-scale-hover: 1.05;
    --btn-scale-press: 0.98;
    /* THE SAME LIGHT AS A CARD, AND IT IS THE SAME TOKEN. Owner report,
       2026-08-02: "the shiny animations on buttons are missing". They were.
       styles/surfaces.css gave every card a specular top-light, a border
       brighten and a lift, all from tokens; buttons were left changing one
       background colour, so a card and a button sitting in the same row
       obeyed two different rules about what light does.

       --shadow-card is `--gloss-inset` plus a cast shadow, which is what
       makes a control read as a lit object rather than a coloured rectangle.
       Nothing is restated here: the recipe lives in tokens.css and this file
       names it. If the card treatment changes, so does this, in one edit.

       On a near-black page the cast shadow costs nothing visually at rest —
       rgba(2, 4, 10, 0.45) on #05070E — and the inset hairline is the whole
       "shiny" part. Both step up together on hover, below. */
    /* ── NOT --shadow-card ────────────────────────────────────────────────
     *
     * The card shadow was matched to the deployed /about on 2026-08-03, and it
     * carries a 14px/36px black drop that belongs under a CARD. Measured on the
     * deployed page, live's button has no dark shadow at all: it carries a teal
     * glow and nothing else, `rgba(12,201,168,0.65) 0 5px 16px -7px`.
     *
     * Inheriting the card's shadow made our buttons visibly heavier than live's
     * the moment the card token changed. A control and a surface are different
     * objects and should not share a drop shadow. */
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-btn);
    /* ── THE MAGNET, AND WHY IT IS `translate` RATHER THAN `transform` ───────
     *
     * Restored 2026-08-03 on the owner's instruction. src/scripts/magnetic.ts
     * writes --mag-x and --mag-y; this is the only place a button reads them,
     * and the fallback is 0px, so a control with no script is exactly where it
     * was.
     *
     * IT USES THE INDEPENDENT `translate` PROPERTY ON PURPOSE, and this is the
     * whole reason the restoration does not cost anything that already works.
     * `transform` on this control is already spoken for twice — the hover lift
     * sets `translateY(var(--lift-btn))` and the press sets
     * `translateY(...) scale(0.98)`. The legacy magnetic-pull.js wrote
     * `el.style.transform` directly, so an inline style would have beaten both
     * and the magnet would have silently deleted the lift and the press.
     *
     * `translate`, `rotate` and `scale` compose with `transform` instead of
     * replacing it, and each animates on its own clock. So the magnet gets the
     * spring below while the lift and the press keep --ease, which they need:
     * an overshoot on a press reads as a control bouncing away from the finger.
     * Two effects, two easings, one element, no fight.
     *
     * The spring is live's, kept exactly: cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1).
     * The 1.56 is the overshoot, and it is the entire character of the effect —
     * the control passes its rest position and settles back, which is what
     * makes 4px of travel read as weight rather than as a glitch. */
    translate: var(--mag-x, 0px) var(--mag-y, 0px);
    /* Named properties, never `all`. `transition: all` on a control animates
       width during a reflow, which is why hover felt sticky on the legacy
       build. Same reasoning, same list, as .surface. */
    transition:
      background var(--dur) var(--ease),
      border-color var(--dur) var(--ease),
      box-shadow var(--dur) var(--ease),
      transform var(--dur) var(--ease),
      translate var(--dur) cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1),
      opacity var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  .btn--md { min-height: var(--btn-h); }
  /* 44px. WCAG 2.2 target size. Not negotiable downward. */
  /* The small button lives in the nav beside a search trigger and a sign-in,
     where a 9.5rem floor would push the row wider than the header allows. It
     gets a proportionate floor rather than the page one. */
  .btn--sm {
    min-height: var(--btn-h-sm);
    --btn-min-w: 7rem;
  }

  /*
   * ── THE PRIMARY'S ACCENT GLOW AND ITS TOP-LIGHT ──────────────────────────
   *
   * MEASURED, 2026-08-03, the legacy site at :8092 against the rebuild at
   * :8095, hovering each control and reading computed style. The owner reported
   * that hover work had been lost since 31 July. Most of that turned out to be
   * wrong — the rebuild lifts 4px against legacy's best 2px, its cards carry a
   * brightening specular that legacy cards have no layer for at all, and it has
   * 2 button recipes against legacy's 19. TWO INGREDIENTS WERE GENUINELY GONE,
   * and both of them were on this control:
   *
   *   ACCENT GLOW   legacy a.nt-btn-primary  rgba(45,212,191,0.4) 0 0 30px
   *                                       -> rgba(45,212,191,0.6) 0 0 45px
   *                 rebuild .btn--primary   no coloured layer at any state,
   *                                         only the neutral rgba(2,4,10,..)
   *                                         drop every surface gets.
   *
   *   TOP-LIGHT     legacy  inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,0.40) -> 0.60
   *                 rebuild inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255,255,255,0.07) -> 0.16
   *
   * WHY THE SHARED TOKEN IS NOT WRONG AND THIS IS STILL NEEDED. --gloss-inset
   * and --shadow-card are tuned for a NEAR-BLACK surface, where 0.07 white is a
   * visible hairline. On --c-interactive-fill, which is the brightest fill on
   * the site, the same 0.07 is invisible: the ratio between the highlight and
   * the fill is what the eye reads, not the alpha. So this is not a second
   * recipe competing with the ladder, it is the same ladder restated at the
   * light level a bright fill needs. The card token stays untouched and every
   * other surface keeps it.
   *
   * NO HARDCODED COLOUR AND NO HUE NAME. The glow is color-mix'd from
   * --c-interactive rather than retyped, and the highlight comes off --c-text
   * the same way .btn--secondary's border already does. If the palette moves,
   * this moves with it — which after the 2026-08-04 role pass means it moves
   * from tokens.css alone.
   *
   * THE FILL IS NOT A MARK, AND UNDER V6 IT CANNOT BE. It used to be solid
   * --c-teal, which meant the site's loudest control wore the colour that means
   * "verified" and asserted something no button can assert. V6 puts the control
   * set on white: the fill is --c-interactive-fill and the glow is the same
   * white spilling past its own edge, which asserts nothing the fill has not
   * already asserted. It could not be applied to .btn--secondary for that
   * reason, and it is not.
   *
   * --shadow-card LAST, and its own dim inset sits under the bright one above
   * it: earlier layers paint on top, so the 0.40 highlight is what is seen. It
   * is named rather than expanded so the drop geometry stays in one place.
   */
  .btn--primary {
    /* Live fills the primary with a vertical RAMP rather than a flat colour —
       measured on the deployed /about — and that structure survives V6 even
       though both its stops moved. --grad-action holds it, so the one thing a
       button knows about its fill is that it is the filled-control ramp. The
       specular inset stays: it is ours, live has no equivalent, and it is the
       "glossy" the owner has asked for twice.

       THE LABEL INVERTS TO THE PAGE, not to a neutral dark. A white chip is a
       hole cut in the page and the label showing through it is the page: 20.13:1
       on --c-interactive-fill, and it stays legible on the pressed state too
       because --c-interactive-fill-lit is one step of the ink ladder, not a
       different colour. */
    background-image: var(--grad-action);
    color: var(--c-interactive-fill-text);
    box-shadow:
      inset 0 1px 0 color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 40%, transparent),
      var(--shadow-btn);
  }

  /* ── THE SWEEP ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * Owner instruction, 2026-08-03: *"Chromium / Shiny Button Effect. Restore
   * with a refined implementation."*
   *
   * THIS IS NEW WORK, NOT A RESTORATION, AND THE RECORD SHOULD SAY SO. G17
   * measured the pre-31-July button in a browser against commit `85024ffc`
   * rather than reading its stylesheet, and the finding was unambiguous: the
   * transition, the rest shadow, the fill ramp, the hover lift, the teal glow,
   * the 1px ring, the inset gloss step and the press are all real and all now
   * present in this component. There was NO sheen and NO shimmer, ever, despite
   * `.btn` carrying `overflow: hidden; position: relative`, which is exactly
   * what a sweep needs and is presumably why the effect is remembered. So the
   * owner is asking for something the site should have had, not for something
   * it lost, and building it as though it were a restoration would put a false
   * claim in the git history.
   *
   * ── EVERY BUTTON, NOT ONLY THE PRIMARY. OWNER DECISION, 2026-08-04 ────────
   *
   * *"button hoover and card hoover must be same like version of website we had
   * before 25th"*. Measured on the deployed site and on both pre-26-July builds,
   * every `.btn` carries the sweep; here `.btn--secondary` measured
   * `::after content: none`, so a reader hovering the pair got a light on one
   * control and nothing on the other.
   *
   * THIS REVERSES A DELIBERATE RULE AND THE ARGUMENT IS WORTH KEEPING. It used
   * to read "PRIMARY ONLY. The secondary is a quiet control and a specular pass
   * over it would make two equal offers out of a deliberate one", which is the
   * same argument the accent glow above still makes for staying off
   * `.btn--secondary`. That argument is not wrong; it has been weighed against
   * matching the build the owner is looking at, and live won — as it did for the
   * card lift in styles/surfaces.css on 2026-08-03.
   *
   * WHAT KEEPS THE HIERARCHY IS THAT THE ACCENT GLOW DID NOT MOVE. The primary
   * still carries a white fill, a 45px spill and a 60% specular inset that the
   * secondary has no equivalent for. The sweep is the same light on both because
   * it is a property of the SURFACE — a pass of light over a raised object — and
   * both controls are raised objects. Emphasis is carried by the fill, which is
   * where it can actually be read at rest rather than only under a pointer.
   *
   * NO COLLISION WITH THE CTA PULSE, and this was checked rather than assumed.
   * styles/effects.css draws that pulse on `.fc .btn--primary::before`. This is
   * `::after`, so they are two different layers on one element rather than two
   * rules fighting over one — and they cannot merge per-property the way a
   * component's scoped rule merges with a shared one. Both sit at `z-index: -1`
   * inside the context `.btn` now establishes, where `::after` paints above
   * `::before` by document order: the glow spills outside the control and the
   * band crosses its face, which is the order a reader would expect. The gate
   * asserts both pseudo-elements exist on that one button at once.
   *
   * background-position, NOT A MOVING CHILD, and that is the whole reason this
   * composes. The obvious build is an inner element translating across a box
   * with `overflow: hidden` — but `overflow: hidden` on `.btn` would clip the
   * CTA pulse in effects.css, whose glow deliberately sits 2px PROUD of the
   * control. Animating the background position of the pseudo-element instead
   * needs no overflow at all, and a background is clipped to the border radius
   * for free, so the sweep follows the pill without a second radius value
   * written anywhere.
   *
   * z-index: -1 PUTS IT UNDER THE LABEL. CSS paints negative-z children after
   * the element background and before inline content, so the light passes over
   * the control's fill and under the text. At z-index 0 or auto it would pass
   * over the label instead and wash it out at the exact moment a reader is
   * about to click it. (That sentence said "the teal fill" until 2026-08-04 and
   * had been wrong since V6 landed: the primary's fill is white now.)
   *
   * ONCE, ON HOVER, AND NOT ON A LOOP. A button that shines on its own is a
   * button competing with the page for attention it has not been given. This
   * answers a pointer, which is the same contract as the lift and the press.
   *
   * ── O-37 LOOKED AT THIS SWEEP AND CHANGED NOTHING, WHICH IS A FINDING ─────
   *
   * Owner decision O-37, 2026-08-04: *"sheen -> white. V6 is the thing
   * shipping; live is the thing being replaced. A single teal element in an
   * otherwise white hover is the anomaly you'd fix next week anyway."* The
   * decision covers the card sheen AND this sweep, so both were read.
   *
   * The card sheen in styles/surfaces.css had a teal core drawn from
   * `--c-ambient-start` and moved to `--c-interactive`. This band never had
   * one: it has been `--c-text` at 50% since it was written, which resolves to
   * #FFFFFF on the dark page, so the button was ALREADY what O-37 asks for and
   * a change here would have been churn dressed as compliance.
   *
   * IT STAYS ON `--c-text` RATHER THAN FOLLOWING THE CARD ONTO
   * `--c-interactive`, and the reason is that the two tokens are the same
   * #FFFFFF today, so the move would be invisible while quietly splitting the
   * site's one convention for specular white — this sweep, `.fx-shimmer` in
   * styles/effects.css and the card sheen's shoulders all write it the same
   * way. A sweep is light falling on a raised object, which is the same fact
   * about every surface whatever the control underneath it is for. The next
   * agent who notices the mismatch and "fixes" it should read this paragraph
   * first: it was noticed, and leaving it was the decision.
   */
  .btn::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: -1;
    pointer-events: none;
    border-radius: inherit;
    /* Local, and rule 7 permits it: every colour resolves from a token. The
       angle and the three stops ARE the effect and cannot come from a shared
       value, for the reason recorded on the amended rule. */
    background-image: linear-gradient(
      105deg,
      transparent 42%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 50%, transparent) 50%,
      transparent 58%
    );
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
    /* Wider than the control so the band is fully off one edge at each end of
       the travel, which is what stops it appearing and vanishing mid-face. */
    background-size: 250% 100%;
    background-position: 160% 0;
    opacity: 0;
  }

  @media (hover: hover) and (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .btn:hover:not(:disabled)::after {
      background-position: -60% 0;
      opacity: 1;
      /* The sweep is slower than every other hover property on this control
         (--dur is 0.3s) on purpose: the lift and the glow are the ANSWER and
         must feel immediate, while the light crossing the face is the flourish
         that follows it. Fading in over 0.12s and out over --dur means the
         band is never caught frozen mid-face when a pointer leaves early. */
      transition:
        background-position 0.62s var(--ease),
        opacity 0.12s linear;
    }
    .btn::after {
      transition:
        background-position 0s,
        opacity var(--dur) linear;
    }
  }

  /*
   * ── THE TWO BUTTONS LOOK UNEQUAL AND THE BOX MODEL IS NOT WHY ─────────────
   *
   * Owner report, 2026-08-03: the green and black buttons look different sizes.
   * Measured across /, /about, /contact, /pricing and /crowmark, every .btn is
   * 54px tall with the same 24px inline padding, the same 15px font and the
   * same 1px border. A ruler says they are identical.
   *
   * WHAT IS ACTUALLY HAPPENING IS IRRADIATION. A bright saturated fill on a
   * dark ground is perceived as larger than a dark fill of the same dimensions;
   * the eye spreads the light past the edge. --c-teal on near-black is about as
   * strong a case of it as this palette can produce. So the primary looks
   * bigger, and it will keep looking bigger however many times the heights are
   * checked.
   *
   * THE FIX IS OPTICAL AND MUST NOT BE METRIC. Changing either height to
   * compensate would make them genuinely unequal in order to look equal, and
   * would break the --btn-h contract, the 44px floor and every row that lines a
   * button up with a field. Nothing below touches height, padding or type.
   *
   * WHAT CHANGED IS PRESENCE. --c-card is rgba(12, 16, 32, 0.55) — within a few
   * percent of the page it sits on — so the secondary was a 1px outline around
   * nothing, and an outline never reads as the same object as a solid. It now
   * takes the raised end of the surface ladder as a WASH rather than a flat
   * fill, --c-lit resolving to --c-raised, which is a real surface a light is
   * falling on. Two things follow: the control reads as an object of the same
   * weight as the primary, and the pair reads as one control system rather than
   * as a button beside a hole.
   *
   * THE BORDER ROSE WITH IT, from --c-border's 0.18 alpha to a neutral 22% of
   * --c-text. Composed from a token rather than typed, and neutral rather than
   * teal, because a teal edge on a secondary would put the interactive marker
   * on the control that is NOT the primary action.
   *
   * ON A PAGE THAT IS NOW A LADDER, THIS IS ALSO JUST CONSISTENCY. Every other
   * raised thing on the homepage — the spine panel, the rail panel, the chips,
   * the closing card — stands on an opaque step. A button was the last surface
   * still translucent over the page it sits on.
   */
  .btn--secondary {
    /* --grad-ramp between two surface steps. A control is a raised surface and
       a raised surface is a wash, never a flat fill. */
    --ramp-from: var(--c-lit);
    --ramp-to: var(--c-raised);
    background: var(--grad-ramp);
    color: var(--c-text);
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 22%, transparent);
  }

  /* ── The lit state ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Identical in structure to .surface's, and deliberately so: the specular
     inset rises from 0.07 to 0.16 and the cast shadow deepens (--shadow-card
     to --shadow-card-hover), the border brightens to --c-border-glow, and the
     control lifts by --lift-btn, which is 1.5px against the card's 2px: live
     moves a control less than the surface it sits on. Same recipe at a higher light
     level, not a second one, and every value comes from tokens.css.

     A BUTTON ALWAYS TAKES THE LIFT, where a card only takes it if it is
     actually interactive. surfaces.css restricts the lift to a, button, label
     and .surface--lift because "lifting a paragraph of text under the pointer
     advertises an affordance that is not there". A button IS that affordance,
     so the exception does not apply and the shared reasoning is satisfied
     rather than sidestepped.

     @media (hover: hover) so a touch device does not latch a control into its
     hover state after a tap and leave it there. */
  @media (hover: hover) {
    /* ── THE HOVER SCALE, RESTORED 2026-08-04 ───────────────────────────────
     *
     * Live grows the control to 1.05 under the pointer and this build had only
     * the 1.5px lift, so a button answered a cursor with a movement most people
     * cannot see while live answers with one nobody misses. It is the same
     * ingredient the card hover was missing, on the other control.
     *
     * IT DOES NOT FIGHT THE MAGNET, and that was the thing to check before
     * adding it. src/scripts/magnetic.ts writes `--mag-x`/`--mag-y` into the
     * independent `translate` property, which composes with `transform` rather
     * than replacing it — see the note on `translate` at the head of `.btn`. The
     * individual properties are applied translate, then rotate, then scale, then
     * `transform`, so the scale here happens INSIDE the magnetic offset: a
     * button leaning 4px towards the pointer still leans exactly 4px, it is not
     * leaning 4.2px. Two effects, two clocks, one element, no fight.
     *
     * GROWING UNDER A POINTER CANNOT FLICKER, which is the usual objection. The
     * hit area only ever gets larger while the pointer is inside it, so there is
     * no state where the growth removes the hover that caused it. That is the
     * difference between this and the autonomous pulse styles/effects.css warns
     * about, which changes size with no pointer involved. */
    .btn:hover:not(:disabled) {
      box-shadow: var(--shadow-card-hover);
      border-color: var(--c-border-glow);
      transform: translateY(var(--lift-btn)) scale(var(--btn-scale-hover));
    }

    /* The same two layers one step up: 30px -> 45px at 40% -> 60%, and the
       highlight 40% -> 60%. Both steps are the legacy figures, and both are
       proportionally the same move --shadow-card -> --shadow-card-hover makes
       on every other surface, so the control is louder than a card without
       being a different idea. */
    .btn--primary:hover:not(:disabled) {
      /* Live's hover, measured: the glow doubles and gains a second tighter
         pass beneath it, and the fill ramp flattens to its far stop. No dark
         drop shadow at any point.

         THE LIT STATE OF A WHITE FILL GOES DOWN, NOT UP, and that is forced
         rather than chosen: the rest state is already #FFFFFF and there is
         nothing brighter to move to. So the fill flattens onto
         --c-interactive-fill-lit, which is the ramp's own far stop and one step
         of the existing ink ladder. The glow and the specular inset still rise,
         so the control gets LOUDER on hover while its fill gets calmer — which
         is what a surface being pressed looks like. See the token's note in
         tokens.css; this is the one place on the site where a lit state is
         darker than its rest state, and it is deliberate. */
      background-image: linear-gradient(var(--c-interactive-fill-lit), var(--c-interactive-fill-lit));
      box-shadow:
        inset 0 1px 0 color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 60%, transparent),
        var(--shadow-btn-hover);
    }

    /* The same wash one step up, so the lit state is the same recipe at a
       higher light level rather than a second one — which is the property the
       whole --shadow-card / --shadow-card-hover pair exists to keep. */
    .btn--secondary:hover:not(:disabled) {
      --ramp-from: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-lit) 78%, var(--c-text));
      --ramp-to: var(--c-lit);
      background: var(--grad-ramp);
      border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 34%, transparent);
    }
  }

  /* ── The press ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * THIS IS THE ONE THING styles/motion.css COULD NOT REACH, AND IT SAYS SO.
   * The sitewide pointer floor restored the legacy platform rule
   * `:where(button, summary, [role=button]):active { transform: scale(0.98) }`
   * (sovereign-primitives.css:44-46), and then recorded honestly that it does
   * NOT show on a `.btn`: this component sets `transform` in unlayered scoped
   * styles, which outrank every cascade layer, so the hover lift held straight
   * through the press. Its note names the fix as "one `:active` rule inside
   * Button.astro ... left for whoever holds that file". This is that rule.
   *
   * IT COMPOSES WITH THE LIFT RATHER THAN REPLACING IT. `translateY` and
   * `scale` in one transform, so a pressed button stays raised and gets
   * smaller, which is what a physical key does. Dropping the translate would
   * make the control fall back to the page on mousedown, which reads as the
   * hover cancelling rather than as a press.
   *
   * THE PRESS IS NOW A LARGER MOVE THAN IT WAS, and that is a consequence of
   * the hover scale above rather than a change here. A pointer user goes 1.05 ->
   * 0.98 rather than 1 -> 0.98, so the control travels 6.7% instead of 2%. That
   * is the right direction: a press that a reader can barely see on a control
   * that has just visibly grown reads as the growth failing rather than as a
   * key going down. 0.98 itself is unchanged, and it is the platform value
   * styles/motion.css restored from sovereign-primitives.css:44-46. A touch
   * user, who never had the hover, still gets exactly the 1 -> 0.98 press they
   * had before.
   *
   * WHY IT MATTERS ON TOUCH, WHICH IS WHERE MOST OF THIS AUDIENCE IS. There is
   * no `:hover` on a phone, so without an `:active` state a button gives no
   * feedback at all between the tap and the navigation. This is deliberately
   * NOT inside `@media (hover: hover)` for that reason.
   *
   * IT IS NO LONGER INSIDE `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference)`
   * EITHER, and it has not stopped honouring the preference. `--btn-scale-press`
   * and `--lift-btn` are both rebound to their identity values under `reduce`,
   * at the foot of this file, so the same declaration computes to no movement
   * without a second copy of the rule. That is the whole point of composing the
   * movement from named values: the preference is handled in ONE place for every
   * state this control has, rather than in a media query per state that the next
   * state forgets to join.
   */
  .btn:active:not(:disabled) {
    transform: translateY(var(--lift-btn)) scale(var(--btn-scale-press));
  }

  /* ── DISABLED ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * THE COMPONENT HAD NO DISABLED STATE AT ALL until 2026-08-03, and that is
   * why the forms had their own button. `.form__submit` carried
   * `:disabled { opacity: 0.6; cursor: wait }` and `.btn` carried nothing, so
   * converting a form to the shared component would have left a submitting
   * form looking exactly like an idle one.
   *
   * It matters because the contact and partner forms disable the button on
   * submit to stop a double send. Without a visible state the only feedback for
   * "your enquiry is going" is that nothing happens, and the natural response to
   * that is to press it again.
   *
   * `cursor: wait` rather than `not-allowed`: this button is disabled BECAUSE
   * work is in flight, which is a different message from "you may not do this".
   *
   * The hover, focus and press rules below and above all carry :not(:disabled)
   * for the same reason a disabled control must not lift under the cursor: it
   * would read as available. */
  .btn:disabled {
    opacity: 0.6;
    cursor: wait;
    transform: none;
  }

  /* Visible focus is a requirement, not a decoration. :focus-visible so a
     mouse user never sees it and a keyboard user always does. The lit state
     comes with it, so a keyboard user gets the same feedback a pointer user
     does rather than an outline on a dead-looking control. */
  .btn:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive);
    outline-offset: 3px;
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-card-hover);
    transform: translateY(var(--lift-card));
  }

  /* A keyboard user gets the primary's lit state too, not a version of it with
     the glow stripped off. Without this, the rule above would replace the whole
     box-shadow list and the accent layer would vanish on focus — which would
     make the one control a keyboard user is looking at the only one not lit. */
  .btn--primary:focus-visible {
    box-shadow:
      inset 0 1px 0 color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 60%, transparent),
      0 0 45px var(--c-interactive-glow),
      var(--shadow-card-hover);
  }

  /* ── REDUCED MOTION — REBUILT 2026-08-04, BECAUSE IT WAS NOT WORKING ───────
   *
   * IT READ `.btn:hover, .btn:focus-visible, .btn:active { transform: none }`
   * AND IT HAD NEVER BEATEN THE HOVER. `.btn:hover` is (0,2,0) and the rule it
   * was written to override is `.btn:hover:not(:disabled)` at (0,3,0), so the
   * override lost on specificity and lost silently. Measured with `reduce` set:
   * a hovered button computed `matrix(1.05, 0, 0, 1.05, 0, -1.5)` — the full
   * lift and the full scale, on the one setting that asks for neither. It is the
   * identical defect to A-43 in styles/surfaces.css, found by the same probe on
   * the same day, and the two had drifted apart for the same reason: an override
   * written as a LIST of the selectors that existed when somebody last looked.
   *
   * THE FIX CANNOT BE THE ONE surfaces.css USED. That file moved its override
   * into the `utilities` layer, where layer order beats specificity outright and
   * the state list can stop mattering. Nothing here can do that: Astro's scoped
   * styles are UNLAYERED, unlayered ranks above every layer, and putting an
   * `@layer` block inside this file would push the rule BELOW the very rules it
   * has to beat. There is also no `!important` in this codebase — 2,279 of them
   * in the legacy tree is what the layer contract in tokens.css was written to
   * end — and reaching for the first one to win a fight inside one component is
   * not the exception that contract describes.
   *
   * SO IT ZEROES THE INPUTS RATHER THAN THE OUTPUT. Every movement this control
   * makes is composed from four values, and all four are rebound here. The
   * selector is plain `.btn`, so it ties with the base rule and wins by coming
   * later — it never has to out-specify a state, because it is not a state.
   *
   * THAT IS THE PROPERTY THE OLD SHAPE DID NOT HAVE. A state added tomorrow that
   * moves this control composes from the same four values and is flattened
   * without anybody editing this block. The only way past it is to write a
   * literal distance into a new rule, which is already a design-system defect on
   * its own terms and is what the tokens exist to prevent.
   *
   * THE OLD COMMENT ARGUED THAT A TOKEN "CANNOT BE SET TO 0 WITHOUT ALSO
   * REMOVING IT FOR EVERYONE WHO WANTS IT", and that was true of the token
   * BLOCK: zeroing --lift-card at :root would flatten every card on the site.
   * Rebinding it on `.btn`, under `reduce`, reaches this control and this
   * setting and nothing else, which is exactly the scope the argument wanted.
   *
   * THE LIGHT STAYS. The glow, the specular inset, the border brighten and the
   * sweep's own fade are all untouched: `reduce` asks that nothing MOVE, not
   * that nothing respond. The sweep's TRAVEL is separately gated on
   * `no-preference` where it is declared. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .btn {
      --lift-btn: 0px;
      --lift-card: 0px;
      --btn-scale-hover: 1;
      --btn-scale-press: 1;
    }
  }

  /* Windows high-contrast throws user styling away and paints its own. A
     box-shadow there is either invisible or wrong, and the border is the only
     thing separating one control from the next. Same treatment .surface gets. */
  /* The variant selectors are named alongside the base ones because the primary
     now sets its own box-shadow at rest, on hover and on focus, and a rule that
     only zeroed `.btn` would leave three lit lists standing in high contrast. */
  @media (forced-colors: active) {
    .btn,
    .btn:hover,
    .btn:focus-visible,
    .btn--primary,
    .btn--primary:hover,
    .btn--primary:focus-visible {
      box-shadow: none;
    }
  }
</style>
